# L §4.6 step 3.3: among candidates still tied after 3.2, prefer "a row with a span over one with always".  Rows at
# the same keys overlapping without replace are refused (step 2), so the preference meets rows that state as many
# keys at different positions: x[a, *] (always) and x[*, b] (2026) both state one key and both match (a, b).
# In 2026 the spanned row gives 5; in 2025 only the always row matches: 0.
=== m.tally
model t
  tally 2
  on 2026-12-31
  start 2026-01-01
  end 2026-12-31

source s "s"
index ks = [a, c]
index js = [b, d]
input x[k in ks, j in js]: int

version v1
  cite s
  governs always
  known 2025-12-01
  x[a, *] = 0

version v2
  cite s
  governs 2026
  known 2025-12-15
  x[*, b] = 5

ask q1: x[a, b] law 2026-03-01
ask q2: x[a, b] law 2025-03-01
=== expect
q1: 5
  law date 2026-03-01, cut 2026-12-31
q2: 0
  law date 2025-03-01, cut 2026-12-31
